### src/shims-obs.d.ts
Location: `[PluginName]/org.minsol.[PluginName].Obsidian/src/shims-obs.d.ts`

Required for TypeScript to recognize `.obs` files as Vue components.
This file is identical for every plugin. Do not modify it.

```typescript
declare module "*.obs" {
    import type { DefineComponent } from "vue";
    const component: DefineComponent<
        Record<string, unknown>,
        Record<string, unknown>,
        unknown
    >;
    export default component;
}
```

---

### npm Scripts Reference
```bash
npm run build       # typecheck + full build — must pass before task is done
npm run typecheck   # type check only — run after every file
npm run watch       # dev watch mode
npm run clean       # wipe dist
npm run viewmodels  # generate TS viewmodels from compiled DLL
```

All npm commands must be run via the PowerShell wrapper from the repo root:
```bash
powershell.exe -Command "cd [PluginName]/org.minsol.[PluginName].Obsidian; npm run build"
```

---

## Definition of Done
A task is NOT complete until ALL of the following are true:

- [ ] `npm run typecheck` passes with zero errors
- [ ] `npm run build` passes with zero errors
- [ ] C# project builds with zero errors and zero warnings
- [ ] No ESLint violations
- [ ] No `any` types anywhere
- [ ] No TODO comments
- [ ] No unused imports
- [ ] No console.log statements
- [ ] All public C# members have XML doc comments
- [ ] All shared logic is in utils.ts, not inlined in components
- [ ] No custom types defined inline — use generated ViewModels types
- [ ] Every Bag is its own file
- [ ] Box is only created after all Bags are fully defined
- [ ] Rock core was consulted before any new structural pattern was introduced

---

## Monolithic Repository Structure

All plugins live inside the MinSol monolithic Rock repository. Every plugin
has a parent folder at the repo root containing both the C# project and the
Obsidian frontend project as siblings.

```
[repo-root]/
├── Rock/                                             ← core Rock C# project
├── Rock.JavaScript.Obsidian/                         ← core Obsidian framework
├── RockWeb/                                          ← IIS web root
│   ├── bin/                                          ← compiled DLLs land here
│   └── Obsidian/Blocks/                              ← compiled .obs blocks land here
└── [PluginName]/                                     ← plugin parent folder
    ├── org.minsol.[PluginName]/                      ← C# project
    │   └── org.minsol.[PluginName].csproj
    └── org.minsol.[PluginName].Obsidian/             ← frontend project
        ├── org.minsol.[PluginName].Obsidian.esproj
        ├── obsidian.config.json
        ├── package.json
        ├── tsconfig.base.json
        └── src/
            ├── tsconfig.json
            ├── shims-obs.d.ts
            ├── enums.partial.ts       ← AUTO-GENERATED by viewmodels, never edit
            ├── ViewModels/            ← AUTO-GENERATED by viewmodels, never edit
            │   ├── bags.d.ts          ← flat — no subfolders
            │   └── boxes.d.ts
            └── Core/
                ├── [blockName].obs
                └── utils.ts
```

### Solution Build Order Dependencies
- Plugin C# project → depends on `Rock` (via ProjectReference in .csproj)
- Plugin Obsidian project → depends on `Rock.JavaScript.Obsidian` (set manually in VS)
- `RockWeb` → receives the compiled DLL automatically via `CopyToRockWeb`

---

## Rock Core File Protection

### The Boundary — Read This Before Touching Any File

MinSol is a Rock RMS partner. Rock core files are owned and maintained by
Spark Development Network. Modifying them breaks upgrades, violates our
partner relationship, and creates merge conflicts that are extremely painful
to resolve. This boundary is absolute and non-negotiable.

### What Is Core — NEVER Modify These
Any file that lives outside of a `[PluginName]/org.minsol.*` folder is
Rock core and must never be created, modified, or deleted. This includes:

```
Rock/                          ← NEVER modify
Rock.ViewModels/               ← NEVER modify
Rock.Enums/                    ← NEVER modify
Rock.Rest/                     ← NEVER modify
Rock.JavaScript.Obsidian/      ← NEVER modify
Rock.Lava/                     ← NEVER modify
Rock.Lava.Shared/              ← NEVER modify
DotLiquid/                     ← NEVER modify
RockWeb/                       ← NEVER modify
Rock.sln                       ← NEVER modify directly
Any .csproj outside            ← NEVER modify
  an org.minsol.* folder
```

### What Is Ours — Work Only Here
The ONLY directories Cline is permitted to create or modify files in are:

```
[PluginName]/org.minsol.[PluginName]/
[PluginName]/org.minsol.[PluginName].Obsidian/
```

### How to Identify the Boundary at Runtime
Before creating or editing any file, Cline must verify the target path
contains `org.minsol.` in its directory structure. If it does not,
stop immediately and ask before proceeding.

### What to Do Instead of Modifying Core
If a task seems to require modifying a Rock core file:

1. **Stop immediately.** Do not make the change.
2. **State the problem clearly** — explain what you were trying to do
   and why it appeared to require a core change.
3. **Look for a plugin-side solution first.** Rock is designed to be
   extended without modification. Check whether the need can be met via:
   - A custom block that wraps or extends core functionality
   - A plugin migration that seeds new data or configuration
   - A block attribute that configures existing core behavior
   - A new ViewModel/Bag in the plugin's own ViewModels folder
   - A new service or helper class inside the plugin project
4. **Ask the developer** if no plugin-side solution is obvious.

### Prohibited Actions — Core File Protection
- NEVER modify any file in `Rock/`, `Rock.ViewModels/`, `Rock.Enums/`,
  `Rock.Rest/`, `Rock.JavaScript.Obsidian/`, `RockWeb/`, or any directory
  that is not inside an `org.minsol.*` plugin folder.
- NEVER add a `ProjectReference` or `PackageReference` to any core `.csproj`.
- NEVER touch `Rock.sln` — plugins use their own `.sln` file, not the core solution.
- NEVER copy a core file into a plugin folder and modify it as a workaround —
  this creates a maintenance nightmare on every Rock upgrade.
- NEVER assume a task requires core modification without first exhausting
  all plugin-side extension points.

---

## org.minsol.Common — Shared Plugin

### Purpose
`org.minsol.Common` is the single shared plugin that provides reusable
services, helpers, types, and components consumed by all other MinSol plugins.
It exists to enforce DRY across the plugin ecosystem without modifying Rock core.

### What Belongs in Common
- Shared C# service classes used by multiple plugins
- Shared C# helper/utility methods used by multiple plugins
- Shared Rock entity extensions
- Shared TypeScript type definitions used by multiple plugins
- Shared TypeScript utility functions used by multiple plugins
- Shared `.obs` Vue components used by multiple plugins

### What Does NOT Belong in Common
- Block types — Common has no blocks. Ever.
- Plugin-specific logic — if only one plugin uses it, it stays in that plugin.
- Bags or Boxes — these are always plugin-specific.
- Any code that references a specific plugin's namespace.

If Cline is about to create a Block inside Common, stop — that is always wrong.

### Common File Structure

```
Common/
├── org.minsol.Common/
│   ├── org.minsol.Common.csproj
│   ├── Helpers/
│   │   └── [HelperName]Helper.cs      <- shared C# helper classes
│   ├── Model/
│   │   └── [EntityName].cs            <- shared Rock entity extensions
│   ├── Services/
│   │   └── [ServiceName]Service.cs    <- shared C# service classes
│   └── (NO Blocks/ folder)            <- Common never has blocks
└── org.minsol.Common.Obsidian/
    ├── org.minsol.Common.Obsidian.esproj
    ├── obsidian.config.json
    ├── package.json
    ├── tsconfig.base.json
    └── src/
        ├── tsconfig.json
        ├── shims-obs.d.ts
        ├── Components/
        │   └── [ComponentName].obs    <- shared Vue components
        ├── types/
        │   └── [typeName].ts          <- shared TypeScript interfaces/types
        └── utils/
            └── [utilName].ts          <- shared TypeScript utility functions
```

### Referencing Common from Other Plugins

#### C# Side — ProjectReference
Every plugin that needs Common adds a ProjectReference to its .csproj.
This goes directly after the existing Rock ProjectReference entries:

```xml
<ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Rock\Rock.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Rock.Rest\Rock.Rest.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\DotLiquid\DotLiquid.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Rock.Lava.Shared\Rock.Lava.Shared.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Common\org.minsol.Common\org.minsol.Common.csproj" />
</ItemGroup>
```

Only add the Common ProjectReference if the plugin actually uses something
from Common. Do not add it preemptively.

#### Frontend Side — Path Alias
Every plugin's tsconfig.base.json includes a @MinSol/Common/* path alias.
This is baked into the canonical tsconfig.base.json template. Imports look like:

```typescript
import type { SharedPersonType } from "@MinSol/Common/types/person";
import { formatCampusName } from "@MinSol/Common/utils/campusUtils";
import CampusPicker from "@MinSol/Common/Components/campusPicker.obs";
```

Never use relative paths to reach into Common from another plugin.
Always use the @MinSol/Common/* alias.

### Rules for Working in Common

- ALWAYS check Common before writing any new shared utility or component
  in a plugin. If the logic is in Common already, use it — never duplicate.
- ALWAYS add new shared logic to Common if it will be used by more than
  one plugin, even if only one plugin needs it today.
- NEVER add plugin-specific logic to Common. Common must remain generic.
- NEVER create a Block in Common.
- NEVER reference a specific plugin's namespace from inside Common.
- XML doc comments are required on all public Common members.

### Building Common
When C# changes were made to Common, always build it before the consuming plugin:

```bash
dotnet build Common/org.minsol.Common/org.minsol.Common.csproj
```

---


## New Plugin Checklist — Follow This Order

### Phase 1: Directory and Project File Setup

Never copy files from an existing plugin. Always create every file from scratch
using the canonical templates defined in this rules file.

1. Create all required directories using the PowerShell wrapper.
   Run each as a separate command from the repo root — never chain with &&:

   ```bash
   powershell.exe -Command "mkdir TestPlugin"
   ```
   ```bash
   powershell.exe -Command "mkdir TestPlugin/org.minsol.TestPlugin"
   ```
   ```bash
   powershell.exe -Command "mkdir TestPlugin/org.minsol.TestPlugin.Obsidian"
   ```
   ```bash
   powershell.exe -Command "mkdir TestPlugin/org.minsol.TestPlugin.Obsidian/src"
   ```

   Replace `TestPlugin` with the actual plugin name in PascalCase.

5. Create all project files from the Canonical File Templates section above.
   Substitute [PluginName] in PascalCase and [pluginname] in lowercase.

### Phase 2: Add Projects to Rock.sln and Configure Dependencies



### Phase 3: Write C# Code

6. Create `SystemGuid/` constants for any new block types.
7. Create all Bag files in `ViewModels/Blocks/[BlockName]/` — one file per bag.
8. Create the Box file in `ViewModels/Blocks/[BlockName]/`.
9. Create the C# BlockType in `Blocks/[BlockName]/[BlockName].cs`.
10. Build the C# project using dotnet build on the plugin .csproj directly.
    The ProjectReference entries resolve Rock core assemblies by relative path —
    no Rock.sln or Visual Studio required.

    ```bash
    dotnet build [PluginName]/org.minsol.[PluginName]/org.minsol.[PluginName].csproj
    ```

    CopyToRockWeb in the .csproj automatically copies the compiled DLL to
    RockWeb/bin/ on a successful build.

### Phase 4: Write Frontend Code

11. Install frontend dependencies:
    ```bash
    powershell.exe -Command "cd [PluginName]/org.minsol.[PluginName].Obsidian; npm install"
    ```

12. Generate TypeScript ViewModels from the compiled DLL:
    ```bash
    powershell.exe -Command "cd [PluginName]/org.minsol.[PluginName].Obsidian; npm run viewmodels"
    ```

13. Create `src/[BlockName]/utils.ts` — define all helper functions.
15. Create sub-component `.obs` files — one per responsibility.
16. Create root `src/[BlockName]/[blockName].obs` — orchestration only.

17. Build the frontend:
    ```bash
    powershell.exe -Command "cd [PluginName]/org.minsol.[PluginName].Obsidian; npm run build"
    ```

---

## Terminal Command Rules

- NEVER chain commands with `&&`, `||`, or `;` — this causes "syntax incorrect"
  errors on Windows CMD. Every command is its own separate terminal call.
- ALWAYS use the PowerShell wrapper for ALL terminal commands including mkdir:
  ```bash
  powershell.exe -Command "mkdir MyFolder"
  powershell.exe -Command "cd MyPlugin/org.minsol.MyPlugin.Obsidian; npm install"
  ```
- Execute each command as a completely separate step. Do not combine operations.
- Run all commands from the repo root.
- If a command fails with "syntax of the command is incorrect", the cause is
  almost always `&&` chaining or running a PowerShell command in CMD syntax.
  Retry using the PowerShell wrapper pattern above.

---

## Code Review Checklist
Run this after every code generation session before accepting output.

Ask Cline:
"Review all code you just wrote against the .clinerules. Check for:
- Any use of `any` type
- Any logic duplicated across files that belongs in utils.ts
- Any custom types defined inline instead of using generated ViewModels types
- Any business logic inside Vue templates
- Any missing XML doc comments on public C# members
- Any magic strings or magic numbers
- Any empty catch blocks
- Any unused imports or variables
- Any TODO comments
- Any pattern invented instead of following Rock core
List every violation found, then fix all of them before we continue."

---

## Model Selection Guide
- **Gemini Pro**  → Planning, architecture decisions, Rock core pattern research,
                    and the mandatory pre-code workflow step
- **Gemini Flash** → Executing a confirmed plan, generating boilerplate,
                     and mechanical file creation tasks

Never use Flash for architectural decisions. Never skip the planning phase.
